1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:01,519
One bank.

2
00:00:01,519 --> 00:00:02,823
One coding agent.

3
00:00:02,823 --> 00:00:03,692
Two departments.

4
00:00:03,692 --> 00:00:06,299
Engineering owns a payment reconciliation change.

5
00:00:06,299 --> 00:00:09,341
Fraud and Risk owns a confidential investigation.

6
00:00:09,341 --> 00:00:12,817
The agent is legitimately allowed to help both.

7
00:00:14,117 --> 00:00:17,582
The agent reads both sources and writes a patch.

8
00:00:17,582 --> 00:00:19,507
It may quote nothing private.

9
00:00:19,507 --> 00:00:23,742
But the patch was created with restricted information in its
context.

10
00:00:23,742 --> 00:00:26,052
Can it be shared with Engineering?

11
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:32,340
TrustGraph follows the path: investigation, generation, proposed
file, Engineering repository.

12
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:36,829
Rust checks the source permissions and blocks the boundary.

13
00:00:36,829 --> 00:00:40,820
A human approval cannot grant missing source access.

14
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:48,470
The repair starts a fresh, isolated generation with Engineering
requirements and an approved specification.

15
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:51,645
It does not reuse the restricted patch.

16
00:00:51,645 --> 00:00:55,727
If that is insufficient, Risk must authorize another
specification.

17
00:00:57,027 --> 00:00:59,726
Approval names the exact patch, policy, and destination.

18
00:00:59,726 --> 00:01:03,437
Change any of them, and the old approval no longer matches.

19
00:01:03,437 --> 00:01:08,497
The gate checks before the branch is pushed and the draft pull
request is opened.

20
00:01:09,797 --> 00:01:16,860
Hydra D B stores the connected evidence: people, sources,
transformations, versions, permissions, approvals, and outcomes.

21
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:19,214
The runtime reads it back.

22
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:23,451
Rust evaluates the relationships and coordinates the gated
action.

23
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:27,261
Test policies in the simulator.

24
00:01:27,261 --> 00:01:33,789
Try different audiences, source access, approval lifetimes, and
execution requirements across multiple scenarios.

25
00:01:33,789 --> 00:01:37,304
Inspect the counterexample before activating a change.

26
00:01:37,304 --> 00:01:40,819
The live agent still needs current authorization.

27
00:01:42,119 --> 00:01:49,742
For confidential decisions, regulated code changes, and software
factories, TrustGraph keeps the reason behind each release
inspectable.

28
00:01:49,742 --> 00:01:56,469
The agent can work across departments without everything it
learns becoming available to every department.
